To build a high-performance API for a table with lakhs (hundreds of thousands) of rows, you must avoid fetching the entire dataset into memory at once.
The fastest and most efficient approach using Spring Boot is DTO Projections combined with Pagination.
Why this approach is "Very Fast":
 * DTO Projection: Instead of fetching the Entity (which has overhead for database tracking), we write a query that selects only the columns you need directly into the DTO. This skips the heavy "Entity Mapping" phase.
 * Pagination: We fetch data in "pages" (chunks) so the server never runs out of memory, and the user gets the first set of results instantly.
 * Read-Only Transaction: hints to the database driver that we won't be writing, allowing for optimization.
Here is the complete implementation.
1. The DTO (Data Transfer Object)
This ensures the ID is never exposed and restricts the data transfer to exactly what you need.
package com.fincore.dto;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.LocalDate;
import lombok.AllArgsConstructor;
import lombok.Data;
import lombok.NoArgsConstructor;

@Data
@AllArgsConstructor // Important for the JPQL constructor expression
@NoArgsConstructor
public class GlBalanceDTO {
    private LocalDate balanceDate;
    private String branchCode;
    private String currency;
    private String cgl;
    private BigDecimal balance;
    private BigDecimal inrBalance;
}

2. The Entity
This maps to your specific Oracle table structure.
package com.fincore.entity;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.LocalDate;
import lombok.Data;

@Entity
@Table(name = "GL_BALANCE", schema = "FINCORE") // Assuming table name based on sequence
@Data
public class GlBalance {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "gl_seq")
    @SequenceGenerator(name = "gl_seq", sequenceName = "FINCORE.GL_BALANCE_SEQ", allocationSize = 1)
    @Column(name = "ID")
    private Long id;

    @Column(name = "BALANCE_DATE")
    private LocalDate balanceDate;

    @Column(name = "BRANCH_CODE", length = 5, nullable = false)
    private String branchCode;

    @Column(name = "CURRENCY", length = 3, nullable = false)
    private String currency;

    @Column(name = "CGL", length = 10, nullable = false)
    private String cgl;

    @Column(name = "BALANCE", precision = 25, scale = 4, nullable = false)
    private BigDecimal balance;

    @Column(name = "INR_BALANCE", precision = 25, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal inrBalance;
}

3. The Repository (The Performance Key)
We use a Constructor Expression in JPQL. This is the secret to speed. It bypasses the Entity state entirely and creates DTOs directly from the database result set.
package com.fincore.repository;

import com.fincore.dto.GlBalanceDTO;
import com.fincore.entity.GlBalance;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Page;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Pageable;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.Query;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository;

@Repository
public interface GlBalanceRepository extends JpaRepository<GlBalance, Long> {

    // JPQL Constructor Expression: Selects new DTO objects directly
    @Query("""
        SELECT new com.fincore.dto.GlBalanceDTO(
            b.balanceDate, 
            b.branchCode, 
            b.currency, 
            b.cgl, 
            b.balance, 
            b.inrBalance
        ) 
        FROM GlBalance b
    """)
    Page<GlBalanceDTO> findAllAsDto(Pageable pageable);
}

4. The Service
We enforce a ReadOnly transaction to ensure the Hibernate session doesn't waste time checking for updates (dirty checking).
package com.fincore.service;

import com.fincore.dto.GlBalanceDTO;
import com.fincore.repository.GlBalanceRepository;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Page;
import org.springframework.data.domain.PageRequest;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Sort;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class GlBalanceService {

    @Autowired
    private GlBalanceRepository repository;

    @Transactional(readOnly = true) // Performance optimization for reading
    public Page<GlBalanceDTO> getAllBalances(int page, int size) {
        // Default sort by Balance Date descending to see latest first
        PageRequest pageRequest = PageRequest.of(page, size, Sort.by("balanceDate").descending());
        return repository.findAllAsDto(pageRequest);
    }
}

5. The Controller
The API endpoint accepts page and size parameters.
package com.fincore.controller;

import com.fincore.dto.GlBalanceDTO;
import com.fincore.service.GlBalanceService;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.data.domain.Page;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/balances")
public class GlBalanceController {

    @Autowired
    private GlBalanceService service;

    @GetMapping
    public ResponseEntity<Page<GlBalanceDTO>> getBalances(
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "0") int page,
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "50") int size
    ) {
        // Fetch data
        Page<GlBalanceDTO> result = service.getAllBalances(page, size);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(result);
    }
}

How to use this API
Since the table is large, you should not try to get all 100,000+ rows in one call. Use the page parameters:
 * First 50 records: GET /api/v1/balances?page=0&size=50
 * Next 50 records: GET /api/v1/balances?page=1&size=50
 * Larger chunk: GET /api/v1/balances?page=0&size=1000
Summary of Performance Features used:
 * @Query("SELECT new DTO..."): Prevents fetching unnecessary Entity metadata.
 * Pageable: Prevents OutOfMemoryError by slicing the data.
 * @Transactional(readOnly = true): Disables write-locks and dirty checking in Hibernate.
 * BigDecimal: Used instead of Double to ensure financial precision for the balance columns.
